# Environment / secrets
#
# `.env` is deliberately NOT listed here. It is already tracked (committed in
# 3a90dbe) and holds only the Supabase project ref, URL and *publishable* key —
# all three of which Vite inlines into the client bundle, so none is a secret;
# see the reasoning at the top of .env.example. A `.env` line here would have no
# effect on a tracked file anyway, and a rule that silently does nothing reads to
# the next person as a rule that works.
#
# Everything else stays out. `.env.local`, `.env.production` and friends are where
# a real secret would land, and the SERVICE-ROLE key must never reach a tracked
# file at all — it bypasses row-level security entirely, so it belongs only in the
# hosting provider's environment.
.env.*
!.env.example

# Runtime data store
#
# `data/nav-links.json` holds the header/footer links the owner adds through the
# admin panel (see src/lib/nav-links.server.ts). It is written by the running
# server, so it is state, not source: tracking it would mean a `git pull` on the
# server reverts links that were added since the last commit, and every deploy
# would show up as a conflict on a file nobody edited by hand.
#
# The trade-off is that a fresh checkout has no file, which is why reads fall back
# to DEFAULT_NAV_LINKS in src/config/nav-links.ts instead of failing. Links that
# should ship with the code belong in that file, not in this directory.
/data/

# Competing lockfiles
#
# `bun.lock` is the one committed lockfile and the only one that may be tracked.
# These three are not — a repo carrying two lockfiles has two disagreeing opinions
# about the dependency tree, and whichever one CI happens to read wins silently.
#
# `package-lock.json` shows up here for a mundane reason worth writing down: bun is
# not installed on every machine that touches this repo, so verification sometimes
# runs through `npx`/`npm`, and npm writes a lockfile as a side effect. That file is
# a local byproduct, never a source of truth. Without this rule a single
# `git add -A` commits it, `bunfig.toml`'s 24-hour supply-chain guard
# (`minimumReleaseAge`) stops applying to whoever installs from it, and the README's
# "bun is the package manager" becomes false without anyone editing the README.
package-lock.json
yarn.lock
pnpm-lock.yaml

